// Package trace reads the running program's own call stack, through
// `pkg/std/debug`.
//
// What comes back is a RAW frame: a `Mode` saying how to read it, and a `Loc`
// that is a return address when compiled and a VM function index when
// interpreted. Turning either into a function name is a later phase of the
// library, so nothing here symbolizes anything.
//
// That makes most of a capture unprintable — an address differs per run, and the
// depth of the stack differs per MODE, because the two have different entry
// machinery below `main` (5 frames deep at main compiled, 3 interpreted, at time
// of writing). What IS stable is everything relative: a call adds exactly one
// frame, `skip` removes exactly one, and a buffer too small to hold the capture
// says so. Those are the properties this package exposes and its tests pin.
package "pkg/trace"

// Mode reports which execution mode this code is running in, by reading the tag
// on its own stack frame — a native frame means compiled, a VM frame means
// interpreted. It is the one thing a raw frame says outright, and the one place
// a Binate program can observe the difference: everything else about the two
// modes is defined to be identical. The result is "compiled" or "interpreted"
// (or "unknown", if there turns out to be no frame to read — there always is).
//
// A `const` cannot hold those names, incidentally: `const` in Binate is
// compile-time SCALARS only, and text is a slice. An immutable non-scalar is
// spelled `var x readonly T`.
func Mode() @[]readonly char

// Depth returns how many frames sit above Depth's caller — the caller itself,
// its caller, and so on. The absolute number is not portable (it counts the
// entry machinery below main, which differs by mode); differences between two
// Depths are.
func Depth() int

// DepthAt returns the Depth measured n nested calls further down, so a caller
// can compare depths instead of trusting one. `DepthAt(n+1) - DepthAt(n)` is 1
// on every target in either mode.
func DepthAt(n int) int

// Capture fills a buffer of `size` frames and returns (written, total): how many
// frames landed in the buffer, and how many there were to capture. A total
// greater than the size means the capture was truncated — the contract that lets
// a caller size a buffer in two passes rather than guessing.
func Capture(size int) (int, int)

// Uniform reports whether every frame of a full capture carries the same Mode
// tag, which is what makes Mode's one-frame shortcut sound.
func Uniform() bool
